# Dragonfly.xyz WebGL Effect: Technical Breakdown

## Executive summary

Dragonfly’s hero visual is a **Three.js-driven 3D scene processed through a custom ASCII/pixelation compositor**. The page does not render the dragonfly as a traditional `THREE.Points` cloud. Instead, it renders a DRACO-compressed GLTF dragonfly-and-branch model into an off-screen texture, then converts that image into a grid of glyphs sampled from a dynamically generated character atlas. The result looks like a point cloud because the ASCII raster is sparse, high-contrast, and visible mainly along the 3D form’s lit contours.

The site combines this WebGL layer with independently animated HTML typography. The orange `DRAGONFLY` wordmark and the large `GLOBAL / SINCE / DAY 1` words are **DOM text**, not texture text rendered by WebGL. This distinction is important: the 3D ASCII scene provides materiality and depth; the DOM type supplies the editorial message and blur/focus transitions.

> **Observed runtime:** one absolute-positioned `webgl2` canvas at the hero, using a Three.js `ShaderMaterial` pipeline. Three.js is bundled into the Nuxt application rather than exposed as `window.THREE`.

